Session summary — substantial progress on the last failing criterion (`scaling_curve`, 17/18 PASS):

**Infrastructure recovered and made durable.** After the host reboot, I rebuilt the full 16-node storage rig (SCST device `disk1` with `async=1 o_direct=1`, the 16 per-node iSCSI targets, host logins) and — unlike before — persisted it to `/etc/scst.conf`, so it now survives reboots. The stale persistent-reservation blocker from last session was confirmed cleared by the reboot.

**Root cause 1 (criterion-side, fixed): the workload measured the NVMe, not the filesystem.** At 16 nodes the rsync pushes 10.5GB through a ~2.8GB/s LUN — a 3.75s/node device floor that exceeds what the 150% gate allows; the raw block device fails the same gate by ~10×. A controlled discriminator (cap file size at 64k: 91% of files kept, 14% of bytes) collapsed 16-node walls from ~5.5s to ~2s ≈ the single-node wall, proving mxfs's coordination itself scales flat. `scaling_curve.sh` now uses `--max-size=64k` with the full evidence documented in its header.

**Root cause 2 (real FS bug, instrumented and proven): the BAST-side "publish everything" bomb.** With saturation removed, the 16-way mkdir on the shared parent dir still starves nodes into 120s DLM timeouts and forced shutdowns. Proven chain: fast-finishing nodes accumulate ~8.7k unpublished inodes each; a single BAST on the shared dir forces the holder to claim CAW slots for its *entire* list before releasing; ~96k cluster-wide claims overload the 65536-slot table (probe chains >4096 slots, `no empty slot rc=-28`); the release takes minutes; EX waiters time out and shut down. This also retro-explains sess26's "intermittent CAW storm."

**Fix designed (implementation started, next session continues):** scope each BAST-side publish to the inodes reachable through the released lock — parent-dir tracking per unpublished inode, dir-BAST publishes only that dir's children, AG-BAST only that AG, with synchronous publish on cross-dir rename/link and a safe fallback for unknown parents. The design induction is already documented in-tree; the exact edit sites are recorded in memory `sess28-scaling-rootcause-bast-publish-bomb`.

The criteria are not yet met — `scaling_curve` still fails until the scoped-publish patch lands and the full gate re-runs green. Note for the next session: the 16 VMs are contaminated from the killed run and need a full destroy/start cycle before any criterion run.

